550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2.5 \u2014 INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n\n2.5-1. R&D capitalization: not applicable (no R&D) [Actual].\n2.5-2. SBC treatment: SBC $4.3M FY25 [Actual]. Adjusted FY25 FCF =\n  OCF 185 \u2212 capex 36 \u2212 SBC 4 = $145M (vs reported 149M). SBC = 2.9% of\n  FCF \u2014 immaterial but deducted.\n2.5-3. Operating lease capitalization: FY25 operating leases $47M [Actual];\n  add to debt. Adjusted net debt: LT debt 33M + current portion 7M +\n  leases 47M + finance leases n/a (2023: 1M) = ~87M. Cash 93M +\n  investments (2025: [Unconfirmed], 2019: 279M; use 150M) = ~243M \u2192\n  NET CASH \u2248 +$156M [Estimate]. Very strong pre-deal.\n2.5-4. Brand/data: not material. No adjustment.\n2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC: Adjusted NOPAT FY25 \u2248 (128+4 SBC)\u00d7(1-0.26) = 97.7M.\n  Adjusted invested capital = equity 1,069 + debt 87 \u2212 cash 93 \u2212 inv 150\n  \u2248 913M \u2192 Adjusted ROIC = 10.7% vs GAAP ROIC ~10.4% [Estimate].\n  Gap < 5pp \u2192 no distortion.\n2.5-6. Owner Earnings (Buffett): NI 120 + D&A 45 \u2212 maintenance capex\n  (est. 28\u201335; actual capex 36) \u2212 \u0394WC 19 = ~110\u2013118M [Estimate].\n  Owner earnings \u2248 $7.0\u20137.5/share \u2014 well below the $9.40 trailing EPS\n  narrative; the company's D&A exceeds capex (45M vs 36M), a sign that\n  the owned facilities may be under-maintained [Estimate].\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION (ENGINE 1: TRADITIONAL DCF + MULTIPLES, WITH\n  ENGINE 2 CYCLE OVERLAY)\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n\nStep 0-2/0-4 \u2014 Engine selection and justification:\n- Archetype: C (CYCLICAL) with A (STABLE INCUMBENT) characteristics \u2014\n  skilled nursing is a regulated, occupancy-driven, reimbursement-cyclical\n  business; the pending lease-to-own conversion adds a special-situation\n  overlay. [3 reasons: (1) 10-yr median op margin 5.3% vs FY25 8.5% \u2014\n  margin cyclical; (2) reimbursement policy is the dominant demand driver;\n  (3) the NHI lease expiry is a discrete, repricing event.]\n- Engine: Primary = Engine 2 (Cycle-Adjusted DCF) using mid-cycle margins,\n  cross-checked with Engine 1 DCF and multiples. WHY: single-year FY25\n  FCF of $149M is peak-ish (margin 9.8% vs 10-yr median ~6%), so a naive\n  growing-FCF model would overstate value. ERROR CHECK: a pure trailing\n  P/E approach (24.7\u00d7) embeds the deal hype; a pure DCF from trailing FCF\n  embeds peak margins \u2014 both distort. Mid-cycle FCF \u2248 8.0\u20138.5% of revenue.\n\nForecast assumptions \u2014 sourced explicitly:\n- Revenue growth: FY26E = $1.60B (Q1+Q2 annualized + Q3/Q4 at +4% YoY\n  [Estimate from quarterly trend]); organic +3.5\u20134% thereafter (aging\n  demographics + rate increases, offset by occupancy softness)\n  [Assumption]; $50.5M/5-SNF acquisition adds ~$20-25M rev FY27\n  [Estimate \u2014 web]. NHI portfolio (if deal closes) adds ~$180-220M rev\n  FY27-28 [Assumption, unconfirmed].\n- EBIT margin: FY26 8.8% \u2192 9.2% by FY28 \u2192 9.2% steady (mid-cycle 8.8%,\n  deliberately below FY25's 8.5%? \u2014 note: 8.5% FY25 actual, use 9.0-9.2%\n  incl. deal synergies) [Assumption].\n- Tax rate: 26% [Assumption \u2014 pack says tax inputs broken in FY22; use\n  statutory-ish rate].\n- D&A: $50M FY26 \u2192 $68M by FY30 (deal adds ~$18-19M) [Estimate].\n- Capex: $50M FY26 \u2192 $68M FY30 (maintenance \u2248 D&A; post-deal maintenance\n  higher) [Assumption].\n- WACC derivation:\n  - Rf = 4.7% (10Y UST) [Actual]\n  - ERP = 4.5% [Assumption]\n  - Beta = 0.9 (vendor 0.641 [Estimate] raised for ~0.5B post-deal debt\n    and binary risk) [Assumption]\n  - Cost of equity = 4.7% + 0.9 \u00d7 4.5% = 8.75%\n  - Cost of debt = 6.25% pre-tax [Assumption, secured facility at SOFR+250]\n  - WACC = 8.75% (equity ~85% \u00d7 8.75% + debt ~15% \u00d7 4.63% after-tax\n    \u2248 8.12% \u2248 round to 8.75% for binary risk buffer) [Assumption]\n\nStep 3 \u2014 BASE CASE NO-DEAL (mid-cycle, for reference):\n- FY26E FCF: Rev 1.60 \u00d7 EBIT% 8.8% = 141; NOPAT = 141\u00d70.74 = 104;\n  +D&A 50 \u2212 capex 50 \u2212 \u0394WC 5 = 99M\n- FY27E 110M; FY28E 117M; FY29E 121M; FY30E 127M\n- TV = 127\u00d71.025 / (0.0875\u22120.025) = 2,083M\n- PV(FCF) @8.75% = 91+93+91+86+84 = 445M; PV(TV) = 1,370M;\n  EV = 1,815M; + net cash 156M = 1,971M; \u00f715.66M = $126/share.\n\nStep 3 \u2014 BASE CASE WITH $560M NHI DEAL CLOSING (Base Scenario):\nAssumptions (ALL [Unconfirmed] \u2014 mark):\n- Price $560M, financed 70% debt ($392M @6.25% \u2192 $24.5M int) + 30% equity\n  ($168M \u2192 ~0.72M new shares at $233) [Assumption].\n- Rent eliminated on acquired portfolio: ~$43M/yr [Estimate from L&B\n  \"64% above 2024 rent\" and typical SNF master lease structures;\n  midpoint of range].\n- New D&A $18.7M; property taxes/insurance (triple-net conversion) $5M\n  [Assumption].\n- GAAP net income impact: +43 \u2212 24.5 \u2212 18.7 \u2212 5 = \u22125.2M pre-tax \u2192\n  \u2248 \u22123.8M after-tax (slightly dilutive GAAP) + avoided future market-\n  rent increase (fair market +64% \u2192 +$28M would have hit if the lease\n  renewed at market). The REAL economic benefit = avoiding that\n  +$28M/yr; cash-flow benefit = 43 \u2212 24.5 \u2212 5 = +13.5M/yr.\n- FY27E pro-forma: Rev 1.72B; EBIT 155M; NOPAT 115M; D&A 68M; capex 65M;\n  \u0394WC \u22125 \u2192 FCF = 113M; FY28E 120M; FY29E 126M; FY30E 131M\n- TV = 131\u00d71.025/(0.0875\u22120.025) = 134.3/0.0625 = 2,149M\n- PV(FCF) = 104+101+98+94+91 = 488M; PV(TV) = 2,149/1.52 = 1,414M\n- EV = 1,902M; less net debt ~(392 \u2212 93 \u2212 150 \u2212 43 retained cash flow)\n  = ~106M; equity = 1,796M; \u00f716.36M shares = $110/share.\n\nThe base case \u2014 with my best, explicitly-stated assumptions \u2014 produces\nroughly $110\u2013126/share equivalent. The stock trades at $233.40.\n\nReverse DCF (what the market implies):\nAt $3.655B market cap + ~$150M net debt post-deal = EV ~$3.8B. With WACC\n8.75% and g=2.5%, the market implies sustainable FCF \u2248\n$3.8B \u00d7 0.0625 = $237M. That is FCF equal to 15.6% of pro-forma revenue \u2014\nor ~60% ABOVE FY25's peak reported FCF, sustained forever. Verdict:\nEXCESSIVE / UNREALISTIC unless the NHI deal produces un-disclosed synergies\nfive times larger than any reasonable estimate.\n\nMultiples cross-check:\n- Trailing: P/E 24.7\u00d7 vs 10-yr median P/E (est. 14-16\u00d7) [Estimate] \u2192\n  55% premium to own history for a business with decelerating organic\n  growth (+2.2% / +8.8% latest quarters).\n- EV/EBITDA: EV 3.80B / EBITDA 173M = 22\u00d7 vs SNF-operator peer range\n  8\u201310\u00d7 [Estimate] \u2192 120-175% premium.\n- PEG (trailing, vendor): 0.82 \u2014 but consensus growth is \"not available\"\n  [Aggregator], so PEG is mechanically suspect. If real long-term EPS\n  growth is 4%, PEG = 6.2\u00d7. This is a trapped multiple \u2014 do not trust it.\n\nConclusion: Base case IV = $110\u2013130/share. 50% CI: $105\u2013185.\n80% CI: $75\u2013210. The market has discounted the most favorable deal\noutcome, not the base case.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n\n3.5-1. Competitive response: If NHC converts 32 SNFs + 3 ALFs to ownership,\n  NHI loses its largest tenant count. NHI under activist pressure (Land &\n  Buildings proxy fight [Actual \u2014 WSJ]) needs yield. NHI's alternatives:\n  sell to a REIT competitor, 1031-exchange, or hold-and-lease at market\n  rent. Competitive response magnitude: +1 (moderate). Long run, NHC's\n  owned-portfolio model (majority-owned) resembles large regional\n  operators; no national chain response expected at $1.5B scale.\n3.5-2. Reflexivity: The +122% stock rally itself improves deal currency \u2014\n  a $233 equity price lets NHC fund the $560M purchase with 30% fewer\n  shares than at $175. Conversely, if the deal fails, the stock halving\n  would impair balance-sheet confidence, potentially triggering NHI\n  holdover enforcement and lender caution. The stock price is not\n  exogenous here; \u2014 the market is reflexively pricing its own hope.\n3.5-3. Customer behavior: Private-pay senior living demand is demographically\n  supported (aging US) but affordability-constrained after 2021-25 wage\n  inflation; Medicaid SNF mix stays high in TN. LTV/CAC not relevant for\n  SNFs. Occupancy base rate: 80-85% [Estimate].\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n\nStep 4-1/2. Scenario probabilities (sum = 100%):\n\nSCENARIO 1 \u2014 BEAR (25%): Deal fails / lease resets at market / holdover.\n- Context: financing fails or litigation (NHC in default since Sep-2025\n  [Actual \u2014 Skilled Nursing News]) ends with lease repricing +64%.\n- Annualized rent increase: ~$28\u201345M [Estimate].\n- FY27E NI \u2248 88\u201395M; EPS \u2248 $5.7\u20136.1; FCF \u2248 110M.\n- IV: DCF @ 9.5% WACC, g=2% \u2192 TV = 110\u00d71.02/0.075 = 1,496M;\n  PV = 350M + 1,496/1.57 = 953M \u2192 EV 1.30B \u2212 net debt 0.05B =\n  $1.25B \u2192 ~$80/share. Multiple check: 12\u00d7 $6.0 = $72.\n- Scenario 1 IV = $80 [50% CI 60\u2013100]\n\nSCENARIO 2 \u2014 BASE (50%): Deal closes ~$560M, 70/30 debt/equity, moderate cash\n  accretion, GAAP flattish; organic growth 3\u20134%.\n- IV \u2248 $115\u2013130/share (midpoint $120) [Estimate \u2014 full build above].\n\nSCENARIO 3 \u2014 BULL (25%): Deal closes with favorable terms (seller financing\n  at \u22645.5%; price \u2264 $520M; or equity issued at higher prices); rent avoided\n  $50M+; multiple re-rates to 17\u00d7 as lease-overhang risk is eliminated.\n- FY28E NI \u2248 155M; EPS \u2248 9.5; 17\u00d7 = $162; DCF with FCF 160M,\n  g=3%, WACC 8%: TV = 165/0.05 = 3.3B; EV 2.4B \u2192 IV \u2248 $210 [Estimate].\n- Scenario 3 IV = $185 (conservative midpoint) [50% CI 160\u2013215]\n\nStep 4-3. Bayesian update:\n- Prior (base rate): 65% probability of value-destructive lease reset if\n  no deal; 45% probability of accretive closure [Estimate].\n- Likelihood given available evidence: the Holland & Knight press release\n  (deal in motion), NHI 10-Q (contingent rent clause \u2014 economic rent\n  already partly floating), the company's recent $50.5M acquisition\n  (management serious about buying), and the stock's +122% move (market\n  assigning >60% to deal success).\n- Posterior: Deal-close probability raised to 75% (vs 45% prior).\n  HOWEVER, deal close \u2260 value accretion; post-close GAAP could still be\n  neutral/dilutive. Probability-weighted IV accounts for this.\n\nStep 4-4. Expected value:\n- Expected IV = 0.25\u00d780 + 0.50\u00d7120 + 0.25\u00d7185 = 20 + 60 + 46 = $126\n- T0 price = $233.40\n- Margin of safety = (126 \u2212 233.4)/126 = \u221285%\n- 50% CI: $105\u2013$185; 80% CI: $75\u2013$210 (hand-set from scenario CIs)\n- Expected 12-mo total return (dividend 1.24% + expected price drift\n  toward $126) \u2248 \u221245%, plus \u00b130% scenario volatility.\n- Verdict: MOF = NONE (deeply negative). No margin of safety.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4.5 \u2014 TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n\n- Market-implied horizon: At $233, with normalized FCF $110\u2013130M, the\n  market requires ~10\u201311 years of mid-case FCF to break even (EV $3.8B /\n  FCF $135M avg \u2248 28\u00d7 normalized earnings power \u2192 implies >10-year\n  discounting of the BULL case). Equivalent to the market horizon =\n  5+ years of unbridled success.\n- My horizon: 3\u20135 years.\n- Arbitrage spread: Market horizon 5\u20137y vs my 3-5y \u2192 spread ~1\u20133y.\n  Direction: BEARISH \u2014 the market has extended its horizon beyond what the\n  filing evidence supports. No positive arbitrage; the spread suggests\n  time decay in the stock's favor is negative.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\