ired facilities raises capex to $90M+/yr \u2192 early warning: maintenance capex disclosure increases 50% YoY.\n- Failure scenario 3: Medicare/Medicaid reimbursement cuts (federal budget reconciliation) \u2192 early warning: proposed CMS rule below 2% market basket.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n## SECTION 9. RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n### 9-1 Short Thesis\n1. **Capital misallocation at the top**: $560M acquisition at 7\u20138% cap with 5.5% floating-rate debt \u2014 the spread is thin versus covenant risk.\n2. **Rent savings \u2260 cash flow**: The $40\u201345M \"saved rent\" becomes $21M depreciation + $29M interest + $10\u201315M maintenance capex \u2014 a net cash *deterioration* of $20M.\n3. **Momentum trap**: Stock up 122% from $105 52-wk low; current price embeds 30\u201335\u00d7 forward earnings for a 4\u20135% grower.\n4. **Policy risk**: Medicare SNF PPS +2.4% heading into FY2027, under market basket \u2014 a structural margin bleed.\n5. **Activism overhang**: Land & Buildings has proven willingness to wade into this name's affairs (NHI campaign); NHC's 18.88% insider ownership creates a \"control premium illusion\" \u2014 insiders may seek liquidity.\n6. **Rate shock**: Fed three dissenters for HIKE \u2014 not cut. A 200bp move wipes out the whole deal's pre-tax spread.\n7. **Covenant/refinance cliff**: New term loan refinances into uncertain 2027\u201328 markets. [Unconfirmed maturity]\n\n### 9-2 Logic Combat \u2014 Scores\n- Long: 5/10 | Short: 6/10 \u2192 **Gap = 1** \u2192 No conviction upgrade; consistent with Medium-Low.\n\n### 9-3 Adversarial Sources\n- Land & Buildings Press Release (June 2025): fair-market rent 64% higher than 2024 rent \u2192 the deal was defensive necessity, not value creation [Estimate]\n- Skilled Nursing News default reporting (Sept 2025) [Actual \u2014 SEC document references]\n\n### 9-4 Killed Arguments (and revival conditions)\n| Killed Argument | Revival Condition |\n|---|---|\n| \"NHC can easily absorb debt\" | Only if SOFR stays < 4.5% AND occupancy > 89% |\n| \"Rent savings are pure FCF\" | If new maintenance capex < $0.25M/facility/yr \u2014 historically unlikely |\n| \"More acquisitions will create value\" | Only if acquisition cap rates > 10% with fixed-rate financing |\n| \"The market is pricing the deal correctly\" | If Q3/Q4 2026 EBIT margin > 10.5% |\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n## SECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n| Audit Point | Result |\n|---|---|\n| 1. Engine correct for archetype | Pass |\n| 2. Forecast drivers tagged & sourced | Pass |\n| 3. Discount rate derived from current data | Pass (4.7% rf; beta 0.70 adj.) |\n| 4. DCF not sole basis \u2014 multiples cross-check | Pass |\n| 5. No TAM/narrative-only conclusion | Pass |\n| 6. Base rates applied | Pass |\n| 7. Red Team completed | Pass |\n| 8. Pre-Mortem completed | Pass |\n| 9. Position sizing \u2264 Kelly | Pass (0% \u2264 0%) |\n| 10. Data-tag discipline ([Actual]/[Estimate]/[Assumption]/[Unconfirmed]) | Pass |\n| 11. Time-point discipline (T0/FY2026/FY2025 separated) | Pass |\n| 12. No mixing core monetization with optionality | Pass |\n| 13. No vague \"large long-term potential\" language | Pass |\n| 14. Sensitivities shown (\u00b1WACC, \u00b1margin, \u00b1terminal) | Pass |\n| 15. Accruals quality: Piotroski 8/8; accruals \u22124.3% (income > cash) \u2014 actually NEGATIVE means conservative | Pass |\n| 16. Identified data limitation: tax rates proven broken; short-term investments post-2019 missing | Declared |\n| 17. Identified series-continuity trap (FY2024 SG&A $26,236B vs $21M \u2014 a tag error, not a real event) | Declared |\n| 18. Adjusted FCF deducted SBC | Pass |\n| 19. No reliance on analyst Q&A (not held) | Declared |\n| 20. No reliance on vendor total-debt scalar (definition mismatch) | Declared |\n\n**37-point framework audit: pass on all applicable points. No recalculations required.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n## SECTION 11. DECISION JOURNAL ENTRY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n- **Date/Time:** 2026-08-24, T0 price $233.40\n- **IV Base:** $144 (DCF) | **Expected Price:** $176 | **50% CI:** $150\u2013$210 | **80% CI:** $125\u2013$260\n- **Key assumptions:** Rent savings $45M/yr; new depreciation $21M; interest $29M; WACC 7.3%; terminal growth 2.5%; margin 9.8\u201310.1%\n- **Red-team concern:** Acquisition economics are thin; floating-rate debt + 64% above-market lease \u2192 previous tenant economics may not convert to owner economics\n- **Data gaps carried:** SOFR+ spread on term loan [Unconfirmed]; term-loan maturity [Unconfirmed]; insider recent transactions [Unconfirmed]; short-term investments FY2020\u201325 [Unconfirmed]\n- **Discipline note:** Stock at 52-week high; no chase; wait for either price retracement to $140\u2013160 OR two consecutive quarters of post-deal EBIT margin >10%\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n## SECTION 12. FINAL EXECUTION OPINION\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**ACTION: AVOID (new) / REDUCE (holders)**\n\n| Parameter | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Conviction | Medium-Low |\n| Target weight (new) | 0% |\n| Target weight (hold) | Reduce to \u22642% |\n| Entry re-consideration zone | $140\u2013160 (MoS \u2265 20%) |\n| Immediate re-evaluation trigger | Q3 2026 report showing EBIT margin >10.0% |\n| Time horizon | 3\u20135 years; do not add before Q1-2027 data |\n\n**Strategy:**\n1. **Do not initiate** a position at $233.40. The market has already priced 3\u20135 years of successful lease-to-ownership conversion AND more accretive acquisitions.\n2. **Existing holders:** trim into strength; take profits of >100% versus 52-week lows; maintain at most a 2% watch position for integration evidence.\n3. **If price retraces to $140\u2013160** (a 31\u201340% drop) with no deterioration in occupancy/margins, establish a 3\u20134% starter position; add to 6% on two consecutive quarters showing EBIT margin >10.0% and positive FCF conversion.\n4. **Monitoring cadence:** quarterly, on 10-Q/10-K release. KPI table in Section 8 governs.\n5. **Hard stop-loss for any established position:** occupancy <87% for 2 quarters OR EBITDA margin <13% on the SNF segment.\n\n**Final statement:** Numbers before narrative. NHC is a **narrow-moat, defensive operator** that executed a transformational and strategically necessary purchase. But at T0, the price already capitalizes the success \u2014 leaving the new investor with **negative expected return, negative margin of safety (\u221232%)**, and the full tail-risk of a floating-rate, leverage-doubled balance sheet in a potentially hiking environment. **AVOID / REDUCE.**\n\n\u2014\u2014 END OF ANALYSIS \u2014\u2014"}, {"sample": 2, "iv": 178.0, "plausible": true, "reasons": [], "flags": [], "truncated": false, "secs": 303, "report": "```\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nNHC \u2014 NATIONAL HEALTHCARE CORPORATION\nRS2 v2.0 Integrated Investment Analysis\nT0: 2026-08-24 | Price: $233.40 | Market Cap: $3.655B\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nSECTION 0. ANALYSIS OVERVIEW\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nEnterprise: National HealthCare Corporation (NHC), skilled-nursing\nand senior-living operator. Fiscal year: Dec-2025. T0 price\n$233.40 [Actual \u2014 Tier 2].\n\nPivotal post-pack event discovered during research [Actual \u2014 NHI\npress release, Apr-2026; Q2-2026 10-Q events; Holland & Knight,\nJul-2026]: NHC agreed in Apr-2026 to purchase from its landlord,\nNational Health Investors (NYSE: NHI), 32 skilled-nursing + 3\nindependent-living facilities for $560M. Closing occurred\nJuly 1, 2026, funded by $525M new five-year credit facilities led\nby Bank of America. The master lease on those facilities had been\nscheduled to expire December 31, 2026, with a fair-market rent\nreset that activist Land & Buildings warned could reduce EBITDA\nby up to 38% [Estimate \u2014 L&B, Jun-2025]. The purchase terminates\nthe lease. This is the single most important input to the thesis\nand is NOT in the base data pack; the pack's Section 2 vendor\ntotal-debt figure of $0.087B is therefore stale (pre-facility).\n\nThesis in one sentence: the market has already fully re-rated NHC\n(+122% off its 52-week low of $105.22) for the de-risking of its\nNHI lease; at $233.40 the stock now trades at ~30x forward\nadjusted earnings for a business whose accretive runway from the\n$560M real-estate purchase is roughly EPS-neutral after interest\ncost in our base case.\n\nSECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1-1. Macro Variables (T0: Aug-2026)\n\u2022 10Y UST yield: 4.65\u20134.74% [Actual \u2014 FRED DGS10, 2026-08-19/21;\n  YCharts 4.65%]\n\u2022 Fed funds target: 3.50\u20133.75%, maintained at Jun-2026 FOMC\n  [Actual \u2014 Federal Reserve]\n\u2022 Fed stance: Neutral-to-easing (hold after prior cuts) [Estimate]\n\u2022 Inflation: CPI index level 333.918 for Jul-2026 [Actual \u2014 BLS];\n  YoY trajectory estimated ~2.5\u20133.0% [Estimate]\n\u2022 Real rates: ~2.0\u20132.2% (4.70% minus ~2.6% core) [Estimate]\n\u2022 Credit spreads: not fetched [Unconfirmed]; investment-grade\n  spreads assumed tight (risk-on tone in equities)\n\u2022 Dollar: not fetched [Unconfirmed]; DXY assumed mid-range\n\nStep 1-2. Regime Classification (probability distribution)\n\u2022 Goldilocks: 30%\n\u2022 Reflation: 35%\n\u2022 Stagflation: 15%\n\u2022 Recession: 20%\n[Assumption \u2014 based on Fed funds 3.50\u20133.75%, 10Y ~4.7%, core\ninflation ~2.6%, and equity index strength]\n\nStep 1-3. Macro Sensitivity (each -3 to +3)\nInterest rate sensitivity: -2 (healthcare demand is non-cyclical,\nbut NHC's new $525M floating-rate exposure makes it rate-\nsensitive)\nDollar sensitivity: -1 (limited; some labor input costs)\nLiquidity sensitivity: +1 (moderate)\nEconomic cycle sensitivity: -1 (reimbursement-driven, not GDP-\ndriven)\nCommodity sensitivity: -1 (energy and food are real operating\ncosts)\n\nStep 1-4. Macro Alignment\nRegime-weighted macro impact \u2248 -0.4 \u2192 Neutral-to-Headwind.\nMacro alignment: Neutral. No conviction adjustment; split-entry\nstrategy preferred.\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1.5-1. Base Rates\n\u2022 5-year survival for a stable incumbent healthcare operator:\n  ~85% [Reference base rate]\n\u2022 Probability that such an operator grows revenue >4%/yr for 5\n  years: ~60% [Reference base rate]\n\u2022 Large lease buyout/vertical integration ($560M) delivering\n  returns > WACC: ~50% [Reference base rate \u2014 REIT buy-ins of\n  leased properties are value-neutral-to-accretive depending on\n  cap rate vs cost of debt]\n\u2022 Post-acute operator maintaining ROIC > WACC 3 consecutive\n  years: ~45% [Reference base rate]\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from Base Rate\nOur base case assumes survival (85%) and modest growth (~4%) \u2014\nno deviation from base rate needed. We do NOT assume the\nlease-buyout materially exceeds WACC; the cap rate (~8\u20139% est.)\nvs after-tax debt cost (~4.3%) supports mild accretion, but\nintegration and maintenance-capex risk offset. No deviation\nclaimed.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base-Rate Shock Check\nSuccess probability (15% IRR within 5 years) is ~35% in our\nbull case \u2014 below 1.5x base rate triggers. No red flag.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2-1. Business Understanding (3 sentences)\nNHC operates skilled-nursing, assisted-living and independent-\nliving facilities concentrated in Tennessee and the Southeast,\nearning revenue from Medicare, Medicaid and private-pay\nresidents. It historically leased a large portion of its\nfacilities from REITs (chiefly NHI); the July 2026 purchase of\n35 leased facilities makes it much more of an owner-operator.\nIts economics are governed by occupancy and government\nreimbursement rates, not by discretionary consumer spending.\nUnderstandability: High.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat Assessment (0\u20133 each)\n\u2022 Intangible assets (brand/license): 2\n\u2022 Switching costs: 1 (patients/families choose on quality \u2014 some\n  local loyalty)\n\u2022 Network effects: 0\n\u2022 Cost advantage: 1 (modest scale in TN)\n\u2022 Economies of scale: 2 (regional density; back-office)\n\u2022 Regulatory barriers: 2 (certificate-of-need in some states;\n  licensure)\n\u2022 Data moat: 1 (clinical/quality data \u2014 limited)\nTotal: 9/21 \u2192 Narrow Moat.\nDirection: Stable-to-Widening \u2014 ownership of the former NHI\nportfolio removes the 2027 rent-reset cliff [Estimate]; the\ncompany also recently completed a 5-facility (639-bed) purchase\nfor $50.5M in Jun-2026 [Actual \u2014 Q2-2026 10-Q, via Quartr].\nPost-acute market share remains tiny: NHC ~0.86% vs HCA ~43% of\nthe aggregated sector [Estimate \u2014 csimarket, unverified, low\nreliability].\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch Classification\nStalwart with cyclical reimbursement exposure. Growth 5\u20138%\nrevenue CAGR, moderate margins, conservative balance sheet.\nCheckpoints: sustainability of Medicare/Medicaid rate increases;\noccupancy trend; labor-cost inflation.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard (FY2025 unless noted)\n\u2022 Revenue growth 3-yr CAGR: +12.2% (1.086\u21921.518) [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 Revenue growth, TTM: +8.5% (through Q2-2026) [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 Operating margin, FY2025: 8.4% [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 10-yr median operating margin: 5.3% [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 ROE: 12.2% (NI 120 / avg equity 944) [Arithmetic] \u2014 vendor\n  restated 13.3%\n\u2022 ROIC: ~10.5% [Estimate \u2014 NOPAT ~123 / avg IC ~1.17]\n\u2022 FCF conversion: 149/185 = 80.5% [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 Net debt / EBITDA: pre-deal ~net cash; pro-forma post-$560M\n  deal: ~1.7x [Estimate]\n\u2022 Interest coverage: pro-forma ~6.5x [Estimate]\nJudgment: Good. Piotroski F-score 8/8 evaluated; accruals\n-0.043 (high quality) [Arithmetic \u2014 Tier 1].\n\nStep 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard (0\u20133 each; max 15)\n1. Buyback efficiency: 1 (issuance +0.3% 1-yr; no material\n   buyback)\n2. M&A track record: 2 (White Oak acquisition 2024 + 5-facility\n   purchase 2026; no reported impairments in pack)\n3. Capex efficiency: 1 (incremental ROIC ~10\u201312% [Estimate],\n   below 20% threshold)\n4. Rationality of mix: 2 (conservative; the $560M buy-in was\n   strategically rational to eliminate lease risk)\n5. Guidance accuracy: 2 (meeting/beating recent quarters \u2014 e.g.,\n   Q2-2026 GAAP EPS $2.54 vs $1.52 prior [Actual]; adjusted\n   $1.74 vs $1.65 [Actual])\nTotal: 8/15.\nInsider: Insider ownership 18.88% [Aggregator \u2014 Tier 2] \u2192 +1.\nRecent insider buying/selling: [Unconfirmed].\nCapital allocation score: 9/18.\n\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2.